          "outlet": "Guardian World",
          "url": "https://www.theguardian.com/world/2026/aug/01/spain-pedro-sanchez-calls-for-eu-meeting-ceuta-border-crossing",
          "source_kind": "fulltext"
        },
        {
          "id": 8,
          "cluster_id": 0,
          "text": "Italian Foreign Minister Antonio Tajani blamed the situation in Ceuta on the Spanish government's prior scheme to regularise undocumented migrants.",
          "kind": "quote",
          "outlet": "Guardian World",
          "url": "https://www.theguardian.com/world/2026/aug/01/spain-pedro-sanchez-calls-for-eu-meeting-ceuta-border-crossing",
          "source_kind": "fulltext"
        },
        {
          "id": 9,
          "cluster_id": 0,
          "text": "U.S. President Donald Trump described the crossing of migrants into Ceuta as a 'catastrophe' and an 'invasion'.",
          "kind": "quote",
          "outlet": "NPR World",
          "url": "https://www.npr.org/2026/08/01/nx-s1-5916330/spain-sanchez-ceuta-migrants",
          "source_kind": "fulltext"
        },
        {
          "id": 10,
          "cluster_id": 0,
          "text": "Ceuta and Melilla form the European Union's only land borders with the African continent.",
          "kind": "background",
          "outlet": "NPR World",
          "url": "https://www.npr.org/2026/08/01/nx-s1-5916330/spain-sanchez-ceuta-migrants",
          "source_kind": "fulltext"
        },
        {
          "id": 11,
          "cluster_id": 0,
          "text": "An estimated 50,000 to 60,000 undocumented migrants crossed into Ceuta from Morocco during the border crisis.",
          "kind": "figure",
          "outlet": "Livemint",
          "url": "https://www.livemint.com/news/world/ceuta-crisis-spain-pm-blasts-selfish-reaction-by-some-eu-countries-calls-for-ministers-to-meet-11785603669440.html",
          "source_kind": "fulltext"
        },
        {
          "id": 12,
          "cluster_id": 0,
          "text": "Spain's Foreign Minister José Manuel Albares stated on social media that virtually all those who entered Ceuta had returned to Morocco.",
